<h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
<p>Design partner onboarding backwards from the first deal. Decide what a new partner's salespeople must know and do to close one, teach only that at the start, and give them a short list of target customers, ready-to-use materials and a named person on your side. Then work the first opportunities with them. Onboarding is finished when the partner has closed a deal, not when they have watched the training.</p>

<h2 id="why-onboarding-fails">Why most onboarding stalls</h2>
<p>Many vendors treat onboarding as an information transfer: a welcome pack, a portal login, a long product training and a price list. The partner signs, the team attends a session, and then nothing happens. The product slips down the priority list behind the brands the partner already sells.</p>
<p>The usual causes are predictable. The training covers everything the product can do rather than how to sell it. Nobody agreed which customers to approach first. The materials need rework before they can be used. And the partner's salespeople do not know who to call when a customer asks a question they cannot answer. Each of these is fixable before the partner signs.</p>

<h2 id="define-the-first-deal">Start by defining the first deal</h2>
<p>Describe the most likely first sale in concrete terms: the type of customer, the problem that triggers the purchase, the product or bundle they buy, who is involved in the decision and roughly how long it takes. Then list what the partner's team needs to make that sale happen. That list becomes your onboarding plan. Everything else can wait until later.</p>

<h2 id="the-onboarding-plan">An onboarding plan in four stages</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Kickoff.</strong> Meet the partner's sales lead and the people who will actually sell. Agree on the target segment, a short list of named accounts, the first activities and how you will communicate. Write the agreed actions down and share them.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales readiness.</strong> Short, practical training on who buys the product, the questions that qualify a customer, a simple demo and answers to the common objections. Keep the first session short enough that busy salespeople attend it. Technical certification, if needed, can run alongside.</li>
  <li><strong>First campaign.</strong> Launch a small, specific outreach to the agreed accounts: an email the partner sends under its own name, a webinar, or a joint event. Provide the content ready to use, so the partner only needs to add its logo and contacts.</li>
  <li><strong>Joint selling.</strong> Join the first customer calls if the partner wants you there, help qualify and price the first opportunities, and handle technical questions quickly. Register and protect these deals visibly, so the partner sees the program works.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="the-starter-kit">What belongs in the starter kit</h2>
<ul>
  <li>A one-page guide to the ideal customer and the problem the product solves.</li>
  <li>Qualification questions and a simple way to recognize a good opportunity.</li>
  <li>A short presentation and a product sheet the partner can co-brand.</li>
  <li>Two or three customer emails ready to adapt.</li>
  <li>Partner pricing, the deal registration process and how to order.</li>
  <li>Demo access or evaluation units, with instructions.</li>
  <li>Names and contact details for sales, technical and order support on your side, with response times.</li>
</ul>
<p>If any of these require the partner to do substantial work before using them, they will not be used. Test the kit with one friendly partner before rolling it out.</p>

<h2 id="the-people">The people make the difference</h2>
<p>A named partner manager who checks in regularly, knows the partner's sales team by name and removes obstacles quickly is often the deciding factor in whether onboarding succeeds. Partners notice whether a vendor shows up. Agree on a short regular call during the first months to review the target accounts, open opportunities and anything blocking progress.</p>
<p>Inside the partner, find a champion: one salesperson who is genuinely interested in the product. Help that person win early, and their success becomes the internal case for the rest of the team.</p>

<h2 id="common-mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Onboarding too many partners at once.</strong> A vendor that signs many partners in a single quarter cannot support any of them well. Start with a few and do it properly.</li>
  <li><strong>Training the wrong people.</strong> Technical staff often attend while the salespeople who need to start conversations do not.</li>
  <li><strong>Leaving targets vague.</strong> "Sell to manufacturers" gives nobody a place to start. A short list of named accounts does.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="what-to-measure">What to measure</h2>
<p>Track a small number of leading signs rather than waiting for revenue:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Whether the kickoff happened and actions were agreed.</li>
  <li>How many of the partner's salespeople completed the sales training.</li>
  <li>How many target accounts were contacted.</li>
  <li>The number of deals registered and their progress.</li>
  <li>Time from signing to the first registered deal, and to the first closed deal.</li>
</ul>
<p>These tell you early which partners are moving and which need help, or a frank conversation.</p>

<h2 id="after-the-first-deal">After the first deal</h2>
<p>Use the first win. Share it with the partner's whole sales team, turn it into a short case story if the customer agrees, and use what you learned to refine the target list. Then widen the program: more products, more of the partner's salespeople, deeper technical training. A partner that has closed one deal with your support is far more likely to close the next on its own.</p>
